\fs96 \cf2 Variables\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A variable is a name that holds a value or object. All variables in Mojo are mutable\'97their value can be changed. (If you want to define a constant value that can't change at runtime, see the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/comptime-evaluation/#comptime-values"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 comptime
\fs32 \cb1  keyword}}.)\
When you declare a variable in Mojo, you allocate a logical storage location, and bind a name to that storage location.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  greeting\cf5 :\cf14  String \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 "Hello World"\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement above does three things:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It declares a logical storage location (in this case, a storage location sized to hold a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  struct).\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It binds the name 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 greeting
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to this logical storage location.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It initializes the storage space with a newly created 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value, with the text, "Hello World". The new value is owned by the variable. No other variable can own this value unless we intentionally transfer it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Variable declarations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo has two ways to declare a variable:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Explicitly-declared variables are created with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  a \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 5\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
var\cf14  b\cf5 :\cf14  Float64 \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 3.14\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
var\cf14  c\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Implicitly-declared variables are created the first time the variable is used, either with an assignment statement or with a type annotation:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 a \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 5\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
b\cf5 :\cf14  Float64 \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 3.14\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
c\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Both types of variables are strongly typed\'97the type is either set explicitly with a \cf5 \ul \ulc5 type annotation\cf2 \ulnone  or implicitly when the variable is first initialized with a value.\
Either way, the variable receives a type when it's created, and the type never changes. So you can't assign a variable a value of a different type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 count \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 8\cf14  \cf6 # count is type Int\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 count \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 "Nine?"\cf14  \cf6 # Error: can't implicitly convert 'StringLiteral' to 'Int'\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Some types support \cf5 \ul implicit conversions\cf2 \ulnone  from other types. For example, an integer value can implicitly convert to a floating-point value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  temperature\cf5 :\cf14  Float64 \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 99\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 temperature\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 99.0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In this example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 temperature
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable is explicitly typed as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but assigned an integer value, so the value is implicitly converted to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Implicitly-declared variables\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can create a variable with just a name and a value. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 name \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 "Sam"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 user_id \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 0\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Implicitly-declared variables are strongly typed: they take the type from the first value assigned to them. For example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 user_id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable above is type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , while the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable is type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You can't assign a string to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 user_id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or an integer to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
You can also use a type annotation with an implicitly-declared variable, either as part of an assignment statement, or on its own:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 name\cf5 :\cf14  String \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 "Sam"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 user_id\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 user_id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable has a type, but is uninitialized.\
Implicitly-declared variables are scoped at the function level. You create an implicitly-declared variable the first time you assign a value to a given name inside a function. Any subsequent references to that name inside the function refer to the same variable. For more information, see \cf5 \ul Variable scopes\cf2 \ulnone , which describes how variable scoping differs between explicitly- and implicitly-declared variables.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Explicitly-declared variables\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can declare a variable with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  name \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 "Sam"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  user_id\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable is initialized to the string "Sam". The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 user_id
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable is uninitialized, but it has a declared type, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for an integer value.\
Since variables are strongly typed, you can't assign a variable a value of a different type, unless those types can be \cf5 \ul implicitly converted\cf2 \ulnone . For example, this code will not compile:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  user_id\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 "Sam"\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Explicitly-declared variables have block-level \cf5 \ul scope\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Type annotations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Although Mojo can infer a variable type from the first value assigned to a variable, it also supports static type annotations on variables. Type annotations provide a more explicit way of specifying the variable's type.\
To specify the type for a variable, add a colon followed by the type name:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String \cf5 =\cf14  get_name\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb13 # Or\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 name\cf5 :\cf14  String \cf5 =\cf14  get_name\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This makes it clear that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , without knowing what the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 get_name()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function returns. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 get_name()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function may return a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , or a value that's implicitly convertible to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
If a type has a constructor with just one argument, you can initialize it in two ways:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  name1\cf5 :\cf14  String \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 "Sam"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  name2 \cf5 =\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf15 "Sam"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  name3 \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 "Sam"\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 All of these lines invoke the same constructor to create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  from a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Late initialization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Using type annotations allows for late initialization. For example, notice here that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 z
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable is first declared with just a type, and the value is assigned later:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf19 my_function\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  z\cf5 :\cf14  Float32\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 if\cf14  x \cf5 !=\cf14  \cf18 0\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         z \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 1.0\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 else:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         z \cf5 =\cf14  foo\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 z\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf19 foo\cf5 ()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Float32\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  \cf18 3.14\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If you try to pass an uninitialized variable to a function or use it on the right-hand side of an assignment statement, compilation fails.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  z\cf5 :\cf14  Float32\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  y \cf5 =\cf14  z \cf6 # Error: use of uninitialized value 'z'\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb20 Late initialization works only if the variable is declared with a type.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Implicit type conversion\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Some types include built-in type conversion (type casting) from one type into its own type. For example, if you assign an integer to a variable that has a floating-point type, it converts the value instead of giving a compiler error:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  number\cf5 :\cf14  Float64 \cf5 =\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf18 1\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 number\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 1.0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 As shown above, value assignment can be converted into a constructor call if the target type has a constructor that meets the following criteria:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It's decorated with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @implicit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It takes a single required argument that matches the value being assigned.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 So, this code uses the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor that takes an integer: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__(out self, value: Int)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
In general, implicit conversions should only be supported where the conversion is lossless.\
Implicit conversion follows the logic of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/functions/#overloaded-functions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul overloaded functions}}. If the destination type has a viable implicit conversion constructor for the source type, it can be invoked for implicit conversion.\
So assigning an integer to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable is exactly the same as this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  number \cf5 =\cf14  Float64\cf5 (\cf18 1\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Similarly, if you call a function that requires an argument of a certain type (such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), you can pass in any value as long as that value type can implicitly convert to the required type (using one of the type's overloaded constructors).\
For example, you can pass an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to a function that expects a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  includes an implicit conversion constructor that takes an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf19 take_float\cf5 (\cf14 value\cf5 :\cf14  Float64\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 value\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf19 pass_integer\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 1\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     take_float\cf5 (\cf14 value\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more details on implicit conversion, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#constructors-and-implicit-conversion"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Constructors and implicit conversion}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Variable scopes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Variables in Mojo use lexical scoping. A variable's definition is determined by where it appears in the source code, not when it executes at runtime. The specific scope level depends on how the variable is declared.\
Nested code can read and modify variables defined in an outer scope. An outer scope can't read variables defined in an inner scope.\
Variables \cf5 \ul explicitly declared\cf2 \ulnone  with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  have block-level scope.\
For example, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  code block shown here creates an inner scope where outer variables are accessible to read/write, but any new variables do not live beyond the scope of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  block:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf19 lexical_scopes\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  num \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 1\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  dig \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 1\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 if\cf14  num \cf5 ==\cf14  \cf18 1\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf15 "num:"\cf5 ,\cf14  num\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # Reads the outer-scope "num"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 var\cf14  num \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 2\cf14          \cf6 # Creates new inner-scope "num"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf15 "num:"\cf5 ,\cf14  num\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # Reads the inner-scope "num"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         dig \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 2\cf14              \cf6 # Updates the outer-scope "dig"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf15 "num:"\cf5 ,\cf14  num\cf5 )\cf14       \cf6 # Reads the outer-scope "num"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf15 "dig:"\cf5 ,\cf14  dig\cf5 )\cf14       \cf6 # Reads the outer-scope "dig"\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 num: 1\cb1 \
\cb13 num: 2\cb1 \
\cb13 num: 1\cb1 \
\cb13 dig: 2\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Note that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement inside the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  creates a new variable with the same name as the outer variable. This prevents the inner loop from accessing the outer 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 num
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable. (This is called "variable shadowing," where the inner scope variable hides or "shadows" a variable from an outer scope.)\
The lifetime of the inner 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 num
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  ends exactly where the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  code block ends, because that's the scope in which the variable was defined.\
This is in contrast to \cf5 \ul implicitly-declared\cf2 \ulnone  variables (those without the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword), which use function-level scoping (consistent with Python variable behavior). That means, when you change the value of an implicitly-declared variable inside the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  block, it actually changes the value for the entire function.\
For example, here's the same code but without the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  declarations:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf19 function_scopes\cf5 ()\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     num \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 1\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 if\cf14  num \cf5 ==\cf14  \cf18 1\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 num\cf5 )\cf14    \cf6 # Reads the function-scope "num"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         num \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 2\cf14       \cf6 # Updates the function-scope variable\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 num\cf5 )\cf14    \cf6 # Reads the function-scope "num"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 num\cf5 )\cf14        \cf6 # Reads the function-scope "num"\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 1\cb1 \
\cb13 2\cb1 \
\cb13 2\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Now, the last 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 print()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function sees the updated 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 num
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value from the inner scope, because implicitly-declared variables are scoped at the function level, as in Python, rather than at the block level.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Copying and moving values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Remember that a variable owns its value, and only one variable can own a given value at a time. To take this one step further, you can think of an assignment statement as assigning ownership of a value to a variable:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 owning_variable \cf5 =\cf14  \cf15 "Owned value"\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This means the value on the right-hand side of the assignment statement must be transferrable to the new variable. Here's an example where that doesn't work:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 first \cf5 =\cf14  \cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf18 3\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 second \cf5 =\cf14  first  \cf6 # error: 'List[Int]' is not implicitly copyable because it does\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13                  \cf6 # not conform to 'ImplicitlyCopyable'\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The first assignment is no problem: the expression 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 [1, 2, 3]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  creates a new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value that doesn't belong to any variable, so its ownership can be transferred directly to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 first
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable.\
But the second assignment causes an error. Since the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is owned by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 first
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable, it can't simply be transferred to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 second
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable without an explicit signal from the user. Does the user want to transfer the value from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 first
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 second
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ? Or create a copy of the original value?\
These choices depend on some features of the type of the values involved: specifically, if the values are movable, copyable, or implicitly copyable.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A copyable type can be copied explicitly, by calling its copy initializer or the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 second \cf5 =\cf14  first\cf5 .\cf14 copy\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 This leaves 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 first
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  unchanged and assigns 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 second
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  its own, uniquely owned copy of the list.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
An implicitly copyable type can be copied without an explicit signal from the user.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 one_value \cf5 =\cf14  \cf18 15\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
another_value \cf5 =\cf14  one_value  \cf6 # implicit copy\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 Here, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 one_value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is unchanged, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 another_value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  gets a copy of the value.\uc0\u8232 Implicitly copyable types are generally simple value types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Bool
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which can be copied trivially.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The ownership of a value can be explicitly transferred from one variable to another by appending the transfer sigil (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) after the value to transfer:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 second \cf5 =\cf14  first\cf5 ^\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 This moves the value to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 second
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and leaves 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 first
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  uninitialized.\uc0\u8232 In many cases, this ownership transfer also involves moving the value from one memory location to another, which requires the value to be either movable or copyable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You don't have to digest all of these details now: copyability and movabiltiy are discussed in more detail in the section on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/structs/#making-a-struct-copyable-and-movable"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul making structs copyable and movable}} and in the section on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul value lifecycle}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Reference bindings\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Some APIs return {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/#working-with-references"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul references}} to values owned elsewhere. References can be useful to avoid copying values. For example, when you retrieve a value from a collection, the collection returns a reference, instead of a copy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 animals\cf5 :\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 String\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf5 [\cf15 "Cats"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 "Dogs"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf15 "Zebras"\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 animals\cf5 [\cf18 2\cf5 ])\cf14   \cf6 # Prints "Zebras", does not copy the value.\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 But if you assign a reference to a variable, it creates a copy (if the value is implicitly copyable) or produces an error (if it isn't).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 items \cf5 =\cf14  \cf5 [\cf18 99\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf18 77\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf18 33\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf18 12\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 item \cf5 =\cf14  items\cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ]\cf14   \cf6 # item is a copy of items[1]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 item \cf5 +=\cf14  \cf18 1\cf14   \cf6 # increments item\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 items\cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ])\cf14   \cf6 # prints 77\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To hold on to a reference, use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword to create a reference binding:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 ref\cf14  item_ref \cf5 =\cf14  items\cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ]\cf14   \cf6 # item_ref is a reference to item[1]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 item_ref \cf5 +=\cf14  \cf18 1\cf14   \cf6 # increments items[1]\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 items\cf5 [\cf18 1\cf5 ])\cf14   \cf6 # prints 78\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here, the name 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 item_ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is bound to the reference to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 items[1]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . All reads and writes to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 item_ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  go to the referenced item.\
Reference bindings can also be used when iterating through collections with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/control-flow/#iterating-using-references"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul for
\fs32 \cb1  loops}}.\
Once a reference binding is assigned, it can't be re-bound to a different location. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 ref\cf14  item_ref \cf5 =\cf14  items\cf5 [\cf18 2\cf5 ]\cf14   \cf6 # error: invalid redefinition of item_ref\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more information on references, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/#working-with-references"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Working with references}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0
